<!-- awf:section positioning -->
# {{ .prefix }}-subagent-driven-development

The subagent implementation chain iterates phases. It preserves sequential implementation dispatch and exclusive implementation-tool batching.

<!-- awf:include plan-flexibility -->
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section per-task-review-note -->
**Phase review is report-only.** The parent settles findings after the phase-closing commit.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section when-to-invoke -->
## When to invoke

Invoke when a plan phase benefits from delegated implementation ownership. A plan may mix modes; hand inline phases to `{{ .prefix }}-executing-plans`.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section procedure-resolve-plan -->
## Procedure
<!-- awf:include outline-approval -->

1. Resolve the mutable plan and read every declared phase mode. This skill may run without an effort. If continuity independently fired, validate its immutable slug and exact `.awf/efforts/<slug>/memory.md`, confirm canonical `effort: <slug>` or deprecated legacy `Effort: <slug>`, preserve one user-managed writer, and use the managed worktree when present; otherwise omit effort and memory fields from dispatches and checkpoints. If changed facts newly make continuity useful, invoke `{{ if index .skills "effort-workflow" }}{{ .prefix }}-effort-workflow{{ else }}the effort lifecycle owner{{ end }}` before further mutation. Repository authority remains primary, and standalone memory is forbidden.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-raise-concerns -->
2. You, the dispatching parent, resolve missing phase context, paths, boundaries, checks, or a closing subject when repository authority determines a compliant detail; use the shared authority boundary for a genuine unresolved design fork. Never hand a child an incomplete phase and let it discover the gap.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-extract-context -->
3. Per `{{ .layout.maintainableCodeDesign }}`, preserve the plan's settled durable choices, including a bounded enabling refactor; reassess them if grounded source contradicts them and stop and escalate rather than accept a bolt-on workaround.
<!-- awf:include clean-integration -->
<!-- awf:include implementation-autonomy -->
Extract the complete phase: goal, ordered tasks, exact paths, semantic boundary and ownership, external/internal representations and their translation point, allowed dependency direction, preparatory-refactor decision, prohibited bolt-on shortcuts, validation expectations, dependencies, prior phase commits, checks, closing subject, and the phase's batch of the ADR's declared State changes operations (V2). The owner runs `./awf context <the phase's exact paths>` before editing.
<!-- awf:include context-spill -->
The owner preserves the settled durable design or reports invalidating source facts; does not replan, broaden the task, or perform unrelated cleanup.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section dispatch-conventions -->
4. For a `subagent-driven` phase, verify a known clean and green baseline. A fresh phase owner may run `./awf read plan <plan> <P>` for the executable closure. A task projection's generated scope notice, Phase close, and Advances/Completes outcomes are phase-owner context only; they never transfer commit, review, checkpoint, handoff, helper, or outcome authority; projection changes neither ownership nor checkpoint boundaries. Call one implementation child alone in its parent tool batch{{ if .targetSubagentTools }} with `allowCommits: true`; when operations intentionally target a supplied managed-worktree path, pass that path as `verificationCheckout` while keeping actual mutation paths explicit in the task. Verification identity alone targets the worktree, so parent and child Pi CWD remain the project root; omit `verificationCheckout` for root work{{ else }} and state commit-capable phase-owner mode in the brief{{ end }}, and provide the complete phase, explicitly identify the parent-supplied approved boundary, and, when an effort exists, provide its slug and exact owned memory path. The child is the `implementer` agent, whose rendered contract already governs its authority mode, scope discipline, green obligation, escalation, owner transaction, and return shape, so the brief carries the phase rather than restating that contract. The child never edits parent memory. {{ if .targetSubagentTools }}Choose the smallest reliable tier - `small` (narrow, mechanical), `standard` (substantive but bounded), or `large` (broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence) - escalating after uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope; omit the `model` field to use configured role routing, overriding deliberately with the tier's exact `provider/model-id`. Never pass `default`, `auto`, or `inherit parent` as a model value. Full tier definitions: docs/workflow.md.{{ else }}Choose the smallest reliable tier - `small` (narrow, mechanical), `standard` (substantive but bounded), or `large` (broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence) - escalating after uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope; select the smallest reliable target-native model explicitly, or use the harness default and note in the dispatch brief that explicit selection is unavailable. Full tier definitions: docs/workflow.md.{{ end }} The commit-capable owner performs all ordered work, stages the complete transaction, and creates the declared phase-closing commit, which requires `./awf check staged` and {{ with .vars.gateCmd }}`{{ . }}`{{ else }}the project's gate{{ end }} to pass; a wired pre-commit hook enforces both at commit time, and the owner runs them manually first only in a clone without wired hooks (checkable with `git config core.hooksPath`; when in doubt, run both manually). Never dispatch concurrent implementers or helpers as phase owners.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-status-handling -->
5. Before review, inventory the completed child report and build the phase-review brief with the exact phase-closing commit, complete phase scope, verification results, and verbatim deviation report. Verify that the child worked in the intended worktree, resolve the reported commit as that branch tip, and require `git status --short` to be empty. Dispatch one report-only phase review through correctness, plan/authority, documentation, and maintainability lenses. Require its structured coverage summary to return the exact phase-closing commit, reviewed scope and range, verification results, verbatim deviation report, freshness against the current branch tip, and any unreviewed settlement. The parent owns this transient evidence and revalidates its commit, range, and branch-tip freshness before reuse. {{ if .targetSubagentTools }}Choose the smallest reliable tier - `small` (narrow, mechanical), `standard` (substantive but bounded), or `large` (broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence) - escalating after uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope; omit the `model` field to use configured role routing, overriding deliberately with the tier's exact `provider/model-id`. Never pass `default`, `auto`, or `inherit parent` as a model value. Full tier definitions: docs/workflow.md.{{ else }}Choose the smallest reliable tier - `small` (narrow, mechanical), `standard` (substantive but bounded), or `large` (broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence) - escalating after uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope; select the smallest reliable target-native model explicitly, or use the harness default and note in the dispatch brief that explicit selection is unavailable. Full tier definitions: docs/workflow.md.{{ end }} When deviations or findings exist, the parent lands one focused post-review settlement commit containing plan Notes reconciliation and mechanical or reasoned review fixes before checkpointing or later execution; it never rewrites the child phase-closing commit. A no-deviation, no-finding phase needs no empty settlement commit. The parent owns focused settlement commits and may append only a concise advisory observation to memory. Evidence loss after context loss, session replacement, or effort-free continuation, or any unverifiable freshness, falls back to ordinary terminal review. Divergence, changed authority, reasoned post-review fixes, or any material mutation invalidates affected coverage and triggers renewed review; the report-only reviewer never edits memory.

<!-- awf:include checkpoint-routine -->
<!-- awf:include phase-loop-continuation -->

If an owner stops dirty, inventory `git status --short`, diff, completed and remaining work, and failed checks. Choose parent completion, restore the known green baseline and redispatch the complete revised phase, or stop for user input. An explicit transfer carries the complete revised phase, dirty-state inventory, completed and remaining work, and recovery verification; a blind successor instruction for one task is forbidden.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section per-task-review -->
6. Review is report-only and phase-level; review fixes remain parent-owned and do not recreate the original transaction.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section final-task-adr-flip -->
7. Apply every explicit V2 batch, including the final batch, atomically with matching claims and lifecycle events in the owning phase. Do not flip terminal artifact status in a phase.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section terminal-step -->
8. After all settled phases, reuse only fresh phase coverage: a single phase reviews unreviewed settlement or integration, while multiple phases focus cross-phase composition, settlements, and integration. Always retain `./awf audit` over the complete final range, including settlement commits. Evaluate implementation-review need independently. Invoke `{{ .prefix }}-reviewing-impl` for meaningful breadth, non-obvious logic, contract or compatibility effects, migrations, security, concurrency, data-loss risk, verification requiring substantive judgment, or uncertainty. Skip it only for locally obvious, low-risk, directly verified work where independent review is unlikely to find meaningful hidden consequences. After assurance settles or is explicitly skipped, effort-backed work returns to `{{ if index .skills "effort-workflow" }}{{ .prefix }}-effort-workflow{{ else }}the effort lifecycle owner{{ end }}` for artifact closure. For effort-free work, the parent performs the deferred ADR/plan terminal transaction: apply only legal status transitions under `{{ .prefix }}-adr-lifecycle`, run the required gate and audit, and then conclude.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section notes -->
## Notes
- Sequential dispatch only, never parallel. Helpers remain sequential, commit-disabled, path-disjoint, and parent-integrated.
- Expected output is non-negotiable.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section red-flags -->
## Red flags

Do not casually dispatch over a dirty checkout, infer plan-wide mode, or let a helper own a shared file or closing commit.
<!-- awf:end -->
